                         Patch Metadata - Gen 49/50 - Novelty: 1/2 - Resample: 1/3 - Patch: 1/2                         
╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ mixed_axis_mirrored_ansatz                                                                │
│ patch_description        │ This edit evolves the ansatz by blending a successful hierarchical entanglement scaffold  │
│                          │ with a mixed-axis controlled rotation core and a local entanglement mini-core.            │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1.  **Hierarchical Entanglement:** Reintroduces the non-parametric CZ scaffold from the   │
│                          │ high-performing prior, combining long-range (qubit distance 4) and short-range (qubit     │
│                          │ distance 1) gates to facilitate spatial information flow across the 8-qubit register.     │
│                          │ 2.  **Mixed-Axis Rotations:** Uses a combination of CRX, CRY, and CRZ gates in the        │
│                          │ central parametric core. Mixed axes are generally more expressive in variational circuits │
│                          │ as they help navigate the Hilbert space more effectively than single-axis (e.g.,          │
│                          │ CRZ-only) designs.                                                                        │
│                          │ 3.  **Local Mini-Core:** Implements the recommended local mixed-axis mini-core (CRX on    │
│                          │ outer neighbors, CRY on inner neighbors) using parameter sharing. This increases          │
│                          │ expressivity and local correlation without a significant increase in the total parameter  │
│                          │ count.                                                                                    │
│                          │ 4.  **Symmetry & Efficiency:** Employs mirrored parameter sharing for pre- and            │
│                          │ post-rotations (RY and RZ). This regularizes the model, reducing the generalization gap   │
│                          │ (train-test gap) and improving parameter efficiency.                                      │
│                          │ 5.  **Complexity:** Reduces the circuit depth compared to the current ring-topology       │
│                          │ version (from 114 to ~95) while maintaining a healthy parameter count (86 total           │
│                          │ parameters), aiming to improve the combined score by balancing accuracy, loss, and depth. │
